A||||4||GT Bastard||Need advice from engine-builders||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||04-05-2001||08:41 AM||||Ok, I was origionally planning a 3.1 stroker for my Fiero.  All machine work to the block was completed, and rods were refurbished.  BUT!  I've since decided to use an 2.8 with forged pistons for NOS.  My question is:  Will all of the machine work still apply?  I'm talking about the rods specifically.  When I got them back, they were numbered and the pistons were mated to them(hypereutectic 3.1's.)  I've I have the shop swap to forged 2.8 pistons, is it going to throw off the balance?  I was also considering an internally balanced crank from an '89 cavalier that I've already got.  The only difference I see is that the '89 motor has a crank sensor.  Anybody see any problems with my setup?  I'm a sponge, give me info!!!<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gtbastard.gif">     <P>"My heroes are John Wayne, the Simpsons, and whoever canceled Full House/Bob Saget."||||reg||4
Z||000001||PJ||04-05-2001||09:03 AM||||All the internally balanced cranks have the sensor disk. They also have the larger main journals. Make sure they match up with your block. They changed from 63mm to 67mm sometime in '85.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||04-05-2001||09:54 AM||||According to the Chevrolet Power book, all 85 and later 2.8 liter v6/60 engines with multi-port fuel injection have large diameter (67.25mm)main bearings.||||reg||1
Z||000003||GT Bastard||04-05-2001||10:04 AM||||That was my understanding as well. The block origionally came out of an '85 Fiero SE.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||04-05-2001||01:12 PM||||GTB - Both the 2.8 & 3.1 use the same length rods.  If this is the case, I would think the pistons would be different in that the pin placement would be higher on the 3.1 pistons.  If that is the case, using those pistons w/ 2.8 crank will yield low compression.  You may want to have the machine shop check part numbers and maybe measure the pin height.||||reg||3
